+
+Private runtime lifecycle candidate
+-----------------------------------
+A runtime owns one private UID directory, one never-replaced flock inode and an
+exact PID/start-time/nonce record. It binds one socket under that lease. An
+existing socket may be removed only when the archived exact owner is dead and
+the stored socket device/inode still matches. Unknown/foreign/replaced socket,
+symlink, public directory or live owner refuses startup. Missing state queries
+and idle stop never create a directory, actor, lock or daemon.
+
+Accepted input is durably journaled (atomic replace, file and directory fsync)
+before its API acceptance can be returned. Complete receipt ownership, pending
+commands and current scene validation/intent are one journal snapshot under the
+reservation lock. Journal failure revokes native authority and refuses all later
+requests; no callback may commit from an undurable reservation. Repeated writes
+use one monotonic snapshot number and exact compositor session.
+
+A restart refuses nonempty unresolved journals before listening or constructing
+an actor unless an explicit recovery handler is supplied. Such a handler is not
+authority merely because a journal says validated: fresh native exact family,
+identity/PID, workspace/output and latest receipt proof are still required. This
+revision provides the recovery boundary and refusal, not a production native
+recovery implementation. Normal shutdown stops acceptance, settles existing
+controller semantics through fresh guarded core, destroys clients before any
+external compositor cleanup and releases only its own PID/socket identities.
+A failed actor shutdown is explicit; it is not normal success.
